IS  - 0926-9630 (Linking)
VI  - 290
DP  - 2022 Jun 6
TI  - Towards an Adaptive Clinical Transcription System for In-Situ Transcribing of 
      Patient Encounter Information.
PG  - 158-162
LID - 10.3233/SHTI220052 [doi]
AB  - Electronic patient charts are essential for follow-up and multi-disciplinary 
      care, but either take up an exorbitant amount of time during the patient 
      encounter using a key-stroke entry system, or suffer from poor recall when made 
      long after the encounter. Transcribing in-situ, natural dictations by the 
      clinician, recorded during the encounter, with minimal workflow impact, is a 
      promising solution. However, human transcription requires significant manual 
      resources, whereas automated transcription currently lacks the accuracy for 
      specialized clinical language. Our ultimate goal is to automate clinical 
      transcription, particularly for Emergency Departments, with as an end-result a 
      structured SOAP report. Towards this goal, we present the Adaptive Clinical 
      Transcription System (ACTS). We compare the accuracy and processing times of 
      state-of-the-art speech recognition tools, studying the feasibility of 
      streaming-style dynamic transcription and opportunities of incremental learning.
