PMID- 40570801
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20250906
LR  - 20250906
IS  - 1879-2782 (Electronic)
IS  - 0893-6080 (Linking)
VI  - 191
DP  - 2025 Nov
TI  - SimTA++: Simple attention neural network for clinical asynchronous time series.
PG  - 107735
LID - S0893-6080(25)00615-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.neunet.2025.107735 [doi]
AB  - The task of modeling asynchronous time series data in clinical practice is a 
      formidable challenge, made even more difficult by the multi-modal and irregular 
      nature of real-world medical time series data. Despite advances in deep learning 
      for sequential data processing in other domains, e.g., natural language 
      processing and speech recognition, a solution for clinical scenarios is yet to be 
      fully realized. To meet this challenge, we propose Simple Temporal Attention 
      (SimTA), a new temporal attention module, where the relation between asynchronous 
      timesteps is modeled by a simple time-dependent attention mechanism. We further 
      extend the method into SimTA++, by incorporating a non-linear temporal attention 
      mechanism that captures non-monotonic relationships in temporal data. Our 
      experiments demonstrate the superiority of SimTA++ over recurrent neural 
      networks, graph neural networks, temporal fusion transformers and neural 
      differential equations on three benchmark datasets, including a synthetic 
      proof-of-concept dataset, PhysioNet 2019, and an in-house immunotherapy response 
      prediction dataset. Notably, the proposed method achieves promising performance 
      as a predictive multi-omics biomarker in predicting the immunotherapy response. 
      The source code for our PyTorch implementation is available on GitHub at 
      https://github.com/jingyuerY/SimTA_plus.
