TI  - Automated extraction of colonoscopy quality indicators from free-text reports 
      using a prompt-based large language model pipeline.
LID - 10.17235/reed.2026.11961/2026 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND AND AIMS: Artificial intelligence has increasingly enabled large-scale 
      analysis of clinical documentation, offering new opportunities to improve quality 
      monitoring in endoscopy. In colonoscopy, key quality indicators - such as cecal 
      intubation, bowel preparation, and polyp detection rate - are routinely recorded 
      in free-text reports, which limits automated extraction and large-scale auditing. 
      We aimed to develop and validate a prompt-based large language model pipeline 
      capable of automatically extracting core colonoscopy quality indicators from 
      routine free-text reports. METHODS: A total of 22,577 consecutive free-text 
      colonoscopy reports (2009-2024) from a tertiary care center were used for model 
      development. Performance was evaluated on an independent test set of 537 reports 
      from 2025, manually annotated by gastrointestinal endoscopists. A prompt-based 
      large language model pipeline was developed to identify maximum colonic extent, 
      bowel preparation quality, and polyp detection; colonoscopy completeness was 
      derived from maximum extent. Performance was assessed using accuracy, precision, 
      recall, and F1-score. Quality indicators derived from model predictions were 
      compared with reference annotations. RESULTS: The model accurately classified 
      colonoscopy completeness (accuracy 95%, F1-score 0.95) and polyp detection 
      (accuracy 96%, F1-score 0.96). For bowel preparation quality, overall accuracy 
      was 93%, with F1-scores of 0.99 (adequate), 0.80 (inadequate), and 0.88 (not 
      reported). Multiclass polyp quantification (0, 1, 2, or ≥3 polyps) achieved 
      weighted F1-score of 0.93. CONCLUSIONS: A prompt-based large language model 
      pipeline may facilitate accurate extraction of key quality indicators from 
      free-text documentation, potentially supporting automated and scalable quality 
      monitoring in endoscopy practice. However, performance was lower for inadequate 
      bowel preparation and for some less frequent maximum colonic extent categories, 
      indicating that these clinically relevant scenarios may require further 
      refinement.
